{"title":"Open Frequency","logline":"When a storm cuts power and cell service to the rural town of Red Creek, a small crew of volunteer radio operators must keep a community radio station alive as the only link between evacuees, shelters, and the people still stranded in the dark.","synopsis":"A violent storm has closed the bridge, killed the cell towers, and left Red Creek running on emergency power. At a battered community radio studio, ARIA, NOAH, JUNE, and KELL scramble to restore the signal by hauling a portable antenna up a water tower catwalk, where they relay the station through the hill relay. The effort pays off almost immediately when a faint distress call comes through asking for insulin and dry blankets for the north shelter, and Aria and Noah answer it with the first real sense of purpose the night has offered.

By pre-dawn the four have set up operations in the high school gym, where evacuees sleep on cots beneath handwritten signs for MEDICINE, MEALS, and MESSAGES. June broadcasts road and shelter updates while Kell manages supplies. A teenager named Maya asks the station to broadcast a missing-dog flyer for her beloved beagle COMET, and the crew treats the request as seriously as the medical ones, reinforcing the idea that every voice and every need matters in a crisis.

By morning the studio is warm with bread and wet coats, and the missing dog has been found under a vending machine. The team hangs a sign reading OPEN FREQUENCY and returns to air, with the ON AIR light steady for the first time, ready to keep the town informed for another day.","act_structure":[{"act_number":1,"title":"Setup and Inciting Signal","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - NIGHT","end_scene":"EXT. WATER TOWER CATWALK - NIGHT","summary":"The crew scrambles to restore the station's signal during a storm blackout. Their makeshift relay on the water tower catwalk picks up a faint distress call that reveals the human stakes of the emergency.","turning_points":["Kell arrives with the portable antenna and news that the bridge is closed and cell towers are dead, leaving the hill relay as the only option.","Aria's ON AIR sign flickers and dies, establishing the station's fragile power situation.","Aria and Noah climb to the catwalk and connect the antenna to a handheld receiver.","An UNKNOWN VOICE on the radio asks for insulin and dry blankets for the north shelter, and Aria and Noah freeze."]},{"act_number":2,"title":"Confrontation and Care","start_scene":"INT. HIGH SCHOOL GYM SHELTER - PRE-DAWN","end_scene":"INT. HIGH SCHOOL GYM SHELTER - PRE-DAWN","summary":"The team takes its operation to the shelter, where June manages updates and Kell handles supplies while the community's needs pile up. Maya's request for a lost-dog broadcast becomes a small but meaningful act of trust in the station.","turning_points":["June sets up recording updates at a folding table while cots and handwritten signs organize the shelter.","Maya approaches with a flyer for the missing dog COMET and asks the station to announce it.","June agrees that if they can broadcast insulin drops, they can broadcast a dog, establishing the station as a voice for personal needs.","Noah reports on the radio that the east road is open one lane and heavy trucks must wait until daylight, adding new logistical pressure.","June writes the road update on the whiteboard and takes down the spelling of COMET for the broadcast."]},{"act_number":3,"title":"Resolution and Renewed Signal","start_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","end_scene":"INT. COMMUNITY RADIO STUDIO - MORNING","summary":"Daylight returns to the studio with the good news that COMET has been found. The crew relaunches the station as OPEN FREQUENCY and settles into steady work for the community.","turning_points":["Maya brings the muddy but safe COMET into the studio after he is found under a vending machine.","June delivers the morning bulletin, announcing the open east road, the stocked north shelter, and the recovered dog.","Aria and Noah are seen working the console as the station returns to normal operations and the ON AIR sign glows steadily.","The team hangs the OPEN FREQUENCY sign and settles back to work, signaling that the station has become a lasting community lifeline."]}],"themes":[{"theme":"Community as a Lifeline","description":"The story shows that in a disaster, a community survives by connecting people to one another. The radio station works as a shared space where shelter locations, road conditions, medical needs, and personal requests all receive equal attention.","evidence":["Aria and Noah freeze when the UNKNOWN VOICE asks for insulin and dry blankets for the north shelter.","June says that if they can name roads and insulin drops, they can name dogs.","June's bulletin about the open east road, the stocked north shelter, and the recovered dog closes the story."]},{"theme":"The Power of Being Heard","description":"Voice and broadcast are central to the story. Those with the least power, such as an unnamed caller or a teenager with a flyer, gain agency once the station chooses to put their words on air.","evidence":["ARIA: Give me ten minutes and one miracle.","MAYA: Can the station announce this too?","JUNE: Once is joy. Twice is branding."]},{"theme":"Humor as Resilience","description":"Banter and light comedy keep the characters functioning under pressure. Their jokes about the cursed antenna, the dog, and the miracle show how they endure stress with warmth rather than despair.","evidence":["NOAH: Please tell me that antenna is less cursed than it looks. KELL: It only tried to kill me once.","NOAH: I miss boring weather. ARIA: You miss boring jobs.","KELL: Smart dog. Dry floor, free snacks."]},{"theme":"Small Acts of Care in Crisis","description":"The heroism is ordinary and practical: climbing a catwalk to fix an antenna, sorting batteries, spelling a dog's name correctly, or sharing warm bread. The story suggests that these small acts sustain a community through disaster.","evidence":["Kell sorts flashlight batteries into a tackle box while June records updates at the shelter.","Aria braces the lantern with her boot while Noah clamps the portable antenna to a rusted rail.","Kell enters with a loaf of warm bread wrapped in foil."]}],"narrative_arc":"The story follows a classic three-act disaster-drama shape with a warm, episodic rhythm. Act one establishes the crisis and the crew's technical struggle to restore the signal, and it ends with the inciting distress call that makes the stakes personal. Act two moves the operation into the shelter, where the community's needs multiply and the station's role broadens from information to emotional support. Act three resolves the night's tensions with the dog's return and the station's return to steady broadcasting, closing on the OPEN FREQUENCY sign as a symbol of a restored, shared channel.","genre":"Disaster Drama with Ensemble Comedy","tone":"Warm, urgent, and quietly humorous","protagonist_journey":"Aria begins the story focused on technical problem-solving, asking for only ten minutes and one miracle to get the station back on air. Her journey moves from a narrow focus on equipment to an awareness of the people on the other end of the signal. When the distress call arrives, she shifts from fixing a machine to serving a community, and by morning she is part of a station that exists to give voice to everyone who needs it.","central_conflict":"The central conflict is the struggle to keep a fragile, improvised communication link alive against the storm, the power failure, and the isolation of the town. The crew must maintain a reliable signal so that evacuees and stranded residents can receive medical, shelter, and safety information, while the dead cell network and closed bridge leave them with no alternatives.","setting_overview":"The story takes place over one stormy night and the following morning in Red Creek, a small rural town in a flooded valley. Key locations include a community radio studio running on emergency power, a water tower catwalk where a hill relay and portable antenna are installed, and a high school gym converted into an evacuation shelter. The town is cut off, with its bridge closed, cell towers down, and only the valley's radio infrastructure still functioning.","confidence":0.8}
